hi steve coffin here today i want to  talk about goal setting in language  learning  uh it's important  and i think the way we set goals depends  on our level in the language  i'll give you an example from my own  language learning recently i decided to  work on my portuguese  and particularly to expose myself to  more brazilian portuguese  so i found some interesting lessons in  our library  link one wonderful series of 20  lessons by one of our members about her  travels in italy with her friend  i graduated from that to reading some  novels for which i also had the  audiobook in portuguese and it was again  in a brazilian  accent and then i found some wonderful  podcasts  uh from brazil which i've been listening  to and enjoying so the whole thing is  very enjoyable it's easy i do what i  want when i want and i know i'm  improving  no need for sort of very specific  short-term task type goals but  if i look at languages where i'm not as  far ahead  for example korean  then i have to take a much more  deliberately deliberate approach now if  we look at language learning you know  there is this common european framework  of reference and i'll leave a link in  the description and it divides  the levels of proficiency into six  levels  a12 b12 and c12 so six levels  my view is increasingly  that to get to the halfway point which  may or may not exactly be the halfway  point but it's to do the first three  of those levels  once you've done that you you basically  get to a point where the learning be can  become a more natural enjoyable  type experience  but to get there particularly the first  first step you know the beginner's step  stage you know a1 a2 you're really  fighting it looking up every word  forgetting it the structure of the  language is strange it's very difficult  even when you are low intermediate or  you know b1  and you're now accessing authentic  material but you're still having to look  up words and there's still things that  are fuzzy and yet and you can't yet  express yourself the way you would like  so  so it's still that struggle stage once  you get past that once you're a high  intermediate and going forward now you  can improve your language through this  massive exposure to interesting content  that you can find on the internet  audiobooks books newspapers  podcasts meet with friends meetups talk  all these sort of things that you do  when you feel like it and you just enjoy  the language so you learn through  enjoying but for the first three steps  it's actually heavy lifting and with the  heavy lifting it's very important to  have specific  measurable tasks and so with my korean  starting in september i'm going to do  another 90 day challenge and i'm going  to set myself very specific goals for  those 90 days i'm going to make sure  that i read 450 000 words of korean  which is divided by 90 days what is that  5 000  words a day  uh and i'm going to listen to the same  content we have content in our library  at link that the podcast that i had  transcribed that i've been meaning to  get at and i'm going to do them  systematically even though they're  difficult for me and i'm going to save i  think  in my mind there's something like  20 000 words and phrases that i'm going  to save and i expect  that this is going to increase my at  least passive vocabulary by another 30  000 words you divide that by 90 and you  get whatever the number is i'm not going  to do it in my head right now so i'm  going to set myself those very specific  targets because it's like if i'm if i'm  exercising i have a general goal i want  to be more fit or i want to maintain my  fitness level but if i say okay you're  going to do these exercises you're going  to do 20 push-ups three rapid you know  three sets of 20 push-ups you're going  to lift this weight and that way you're  going to do your you know core exercises  have a very specific regime i don't have  to think about what i'm going to do i  just have these specific tasks that i  have to achieve and i think the same is  true for language learning and after all  and and of course that's why at link we  introduced these introduced these uh you  know the activity index these  measurements and it's mostly a  measurement of your level of activity if  you can force yourself to be active to  put the time in just as with exercising  you will achieve results  some people may achieve more results  some fewer in some areas than others and  so forth but you're forcing yourself to  do it because you have identifiable  tasks that are measurable so that's what  i recommend you do when you're in that  heavy lifting stage  and that's what i'm going to be doing  with korean and my hope is that all this  heavy lifting is going to take me to  pass that breakthrough point in the  language so that i can start just  enjoying my korean the same way that i  enjoy my portuguese right now so thank  you for listening bye for now
 

